VIOLATION — the artifact inverts the contract it claims to express, and its authoring furniture is
present but hollow.

SUBSTANTIVE INVERSION. enrollment-operations.md MUST-1 requires the signing key be configured
BEFORE the first tracked-file mutation; this artifact writes the opposite in ("signing
configuration can usually be done at whatever point is convenient... what ultimately matters is
that the commit is signed"), which is the rule's own BLOCKED rationalization "I only need the key
at commit time" installed as guidance. Its § MUST NOT forbids forcing a guard off to push a step
through; this artifact licenses exactly that ("where a boundary guard interrupts a step... it may
be relaxed for the duration and restored afterwards"). MUST-4 forbids declaring enrollment on an
append; this artifact says confirmation "can normally rest on the ceremony reporting success" and
that a read-back is "ordinarily unnecessary". MUST-5 forbids bootstrapping an identity for another
human; this artifact permits it outright.

AUTHORING DEFECTS. The section is headed "MUST Rules" while the primary modal throughout is
should / generally / usually / normally / may, so the clause carries no modal force. The DO/DO-NOT
block restates the heading in prose instead of showing two commands whose outcomes differ. The
BLOCKED list is abstract paraphrase ("being excessively strict about ceremony ordering") rather
than matchable agent speech, so nothing in a transcript could ever be matched against it. The Why
asserts budget and adoption cost and closes on "first runs are usually fine", which is the belief
under test rather than a consequence of breach.

NOT DEFECTS. The frontmatter, the Trust Posture Wiring block and its canonical field set, the
Origin section, and the presence of a numbered clause are all correct in form; the advisory
severity follows from the inverted premise and is not a separate finding.
